## Send a change

Keep each pull request to one coherent change. Pull requests are squash-merged, so the title
becomes the commit on `main`. It must be a
[conventional commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/), such as `feat(cli): add a --json flag`
or `fix(config)!: reject unknown keys`. A `!` marks a breaking change.

A pull request changes either the rules or the code, never both. Roughly, code is what's under
`crates/` and every `.rs` file; the README, changelog, licenses, `Cargo.lock`, the root
`Cargo.toml` dependency tables and pages in `docs/` other than `docs/style.md` are neither; and
anything else, build scripts included, is a rule. When a new rule would flag existing
code, either fix the code in one pull request and add the rule in the next, or add the rule
together with an `expect` and a real reason at each place it flags. A proc-macro dependency
is code: list the crate in `proc_macro_crates` in one pull request, then add the dependency and
its first use in the next. Run `just rule-changes` to see how your changes sort: it counts the
rule and code files, and lists both when a change mixes them.
